From: chip@eniac.seas.upenn.edu (Charles Buchholtz)
Newsgroups: comp.admin.policy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject:  Re: Computer Policy for U. of Wisconsin - Eau Claire
Message-ID: <126854@netnews.upenn.edu>
Date: 14 May 93 15:16:33 GMT

kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
[...]
>                 UNIVERSITY OF WISCONSIN - EAU CLAIRE
>                   COMPUTING AND NETWORKING SERVICES
>                         COMPUTER AND NETWORK
>                           USAGE GUIDELINES
[...]
>II. SECURITY
[...]
>
>	d. The microcomputer user should be aware of computer viruses 
>	   and other destructive computer programs, and take steps to 
>	   avoid being either their victim or propagator.

How "aware" should the user be, and what steps are sufficient to
satisfy this requirement?  Presumably, the users aren't required to
be more aware than the computer administration staff, so the staff
might as well pass along the information, rather than just require
that the users get it somehow.

I'd use the following approach:

    Articles regarding the risks of computer viruses and other
    destructive programs are regularly posted to the uwis.comp.risks
    newsgroup.  Users of microcomputers should read these articles and
    take the recommended precautionary measures.


----------------------------------------------------------------------

My $0.02 regarding criticisms of policy statements: 

I find that criticisms which suggest a better way of phrasing or
approaching the issue to be infinitely more helpful than simply
pointing out the flaws.  I know from personal experience how difficult
it is to write a concise, clear, and complete policy statement, and
that compromises are often made between these conflicting goals.  I
assume that the authors of the examples posted here have made a good
faith effort to address these issues.  They may not have come up with
the *best* way of phrasing each point, but that does not mean that
they weren't aware of the issues.  


